`_XBF_FUA_FRESH` is only cleared by `xfs_buf_stale` — and the AGI wasn't staled after t=69. So per static analysis the AGI buffer should never be re-read after t=69, contradicting a simple re-read-revert. Before instrumenting, let me test the load-bearing assumption "only test4 touches AG5's AGI" — check whether a *peer* also did unlinked-list ops / fresh-acquired AG5 (which would mean an AG-DLM exclusion failure, a different root).
**Refutation of the single-node theory** (I gave Gemini a wrong premise): **test3 added ino=10485909 (agino=0x95) to AG5's unlinked list at t=72** (P82-ADD), but **test4 removes/inactivates it at t=317**. Two different nodes handle the same inode's unlinked lifecycle. test4's AGI is stale because it never saw test3's add. This is a **cross-node AGI coherency failure**, not a single-node revert. Let me get test4's full AG5 DLM lifecycle (acquire/release/BAST) to see why its AGI wasn't refreshed.
Now the true root is exposed and the single-node theory is **refuted**: ino=10485909 (agino=0x95) is heavily **reused across nodes** — test4 created it (node4_before_20), test3 unlinked it (P82-ADD to AG5 list at t=72), it was freed+reused (gen 279186378→622545939, P103-RELOAD-REUSE-ADOPT), test4 re-adopted it for node4_after_20, then inactivates it at t=317 reading a **stale AGI**. Critically, **test4 held AG5 from t=69 to t=402 while test3 also acquired+modified AG5** — and P88-CLOBBER-PRODUCER shows test4's xfsaild writing AG5 bnobt "while NOT holding AG DLM". This is an **AG-DLM exclusion + AGI read-coherency failure under inode reuse**.

